<section class="content">

<h3>Notes for the Catechist</h3>

<h4>The Christology of Nicaea</h4>
<p>The Council of Nicaea (325) is the starting point of Christian orthodoxy. Arianism said the Son was "of a similar substance" to the Father, but not consubstantial. Nicaea responded: "of the same substance" (<em>homoousios</em>). This is not an abstract theological discussion: if Jesus is not true God, he cannot save us. Only God can reconcile humanity with himself.</p>

<h4>The kenosis as pastoral key</h4>
<p>The kenosis (Phil 2:6-7) is the mystery that most connects with the human heart. God did not come in power but in fragility; he did not dominate but served. This is revolutionary in our culture of domination and success. The kenosis is also the model of the Christian life: "Have the same mindset as Christ Jesus" (Phil 2:5). The imitation of Christ begins here.</p>

<h4>The hypostatic union: a mystery to be lived</h4>
<p>"Hypostatic union" sounds technical, but it is the key to our hope. In Christ, the human and the divine meet without being confused. That means that our humanity can be assumed by God, transformed, divinized. The Incarnation is not a "disguise" of God; it is the marriage between the divine and the human. And we are invited to that union.</p>

<h4>Jesus' question today</h4>
<p>"But what about you? Who do you say I am?" is the question that every Christian must answer with their life. It is not enough to repeat the Creed; we must do it "with the heart to be just" (Rom 10:10). The task of the catechist is not to transfer information about Christ, but to facilitate the encounter with Christ. If at the end of the class the participants do not feel challenged to a personal response, something has been missing.</p>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>Age Adaptation</h3>

<h4>🌱 Seed (ages 7-12)</h4>
<p>Use simple analogies: Jesus is "100% God and 100% man" like a coin that has two sides but is one coin. Activity: make a "kenosis collage" with images of baby, carpenter, cross. Memorize Jn 1:14 with drawings.</p>

<h4>🌿 Sprouts (ages 13-17)</h4>
<p>Connect with the question of identity proper to adolescence. "Who are you?" is the question they ask themselves. "Who is Jesus?" is the question God asks them. Use the image of the "friend who gives his life" (Jn 15:13). Do they know someone who would be willing to die for them? Have they known someone who did die for them?</p>

<h4>🪴 Root (adults new to the faith)</h4>
<p>Many come with a vague image of Jesus as a "moral teacher." Help them take the step to Christian faith: Jesus is not only a model to follow, but God who comes to save us. The step from "admire Jesus" to "entrust your life to Jesus" is the step of the catechumenate.</p>

<h4>🌳 Tree (formed adults)</h4>
<p>Go deeper into the patristic texts: Athanasius "God became man so that man might become God." Irenaeus "The Word of God became what we are to make us what he is." Divinization as the destiny of the Christian. You can cite the Church Fathers and connect with the spirituality of union with Christ.</p>

</section>

