<section class="content">

<h3>Difficult Questions</h3>

<h4>"Why did God become man? Could he not save us in another way?"</h4>
<p>Saint Anselm formulated the classical answer: God could save us in many ways, but he chose this because it was the worthiest of his love. He does not save us by pure force, but by love; and love makes itself close, enters our condition, shares our pain. Moreover, only a human being could represent humanity; and only God could save. That is why we need a Mediator who is both (CCC 460-461).</p>

<h4>"How can someone be 100% God and 100% man? That sums to 200%."</h4>
<p>The coin analogy helps: it has two sides but is one coin. Jesus has two natures but is one Person. He is not "half and half," nor "one part and another part." He is completely God and completely man in every act. The divine does not mix or confuse the human; the human does not limit the divine (CCC 467-468).</p>

<h4>"If Jesus was God, why did he pray? Why did he say he did not know the day of judgment?"</h4>
<p>As man, Jesus had limited human consciousness (called "beatific consciousness" or "infused knowledge" in theology). As divine, he knew everything. In his humanity, he experienced growth, limited ignorance, suffering. All of this is part of his kenosis: he submitted to real human conditions in order to save us from within (CCC 472-473).</p>

<h4>"Why is the divinity of Christ so important? Is it not enough that he was a great teacher?"</h4>
<p>C.S. Lewis posed this dilemma: Jesus is either God, or he is mad, or he is a liar. He cannot be "just a great teacher" if he claims to be God. If he is not God, his preaching is arrogant or delirious. If he is God, his preaching is the most important truth in history. Christians do not worship a teacher; they worship God made man (CCC 426-429, 465).</p>

</section>

